    <!-- 07 The Creator Brief -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="brief">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">07 &mdash; The Brief</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The creator brief</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">A good brief is the difference between usable content and wasted budget</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Include: product info, messaging, hooks, tone, format, do's and don'ts</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Over-scripting kills authenticity &mdash; give guardrails, not word-for-word scripts</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The creator brief is the single most important document in UGC production. A clear, concise brief gets you usable content on the first take. A vague or over-detailed brief leads to reshoots, frustration, and wasted money.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What a good brief includes</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Product info.</strong> What the product does, who it is for, and why it matters. Give the creator enough context to speak about it naturally &mdash; not a marketing deck, but a clear value proposition.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Key messaging.</strong> 2&ndash;3 core points the content must communicate. These are non-negotiable talking points, not a script. Let the creator phrase them in their own words.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Hooks.</strong> Provide 3&ndash;5 hook options for the opening seconds. Hooks are the most important part of any UGC video. Give the creator options and let them choose or improvise.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Tone and style.</strong> Casual, energetic, calm, funny, educational? Reference examples of content you like. Tone is easier to show than describe.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Format requirements.</strong> Vertical 9:16, duration (15s / 30s / 60s), captions on or off, specific platform requirements. Technical specs prevent unusable deliverables.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Do's and don'ts.</strong> What to avoid: competitor mentions, specific claims, banned phrases. What to include: product shown within first 5 seconds, CTA at the end. Clear boundaries without over-scripting.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4 mt-6">The most common mistake brands make is over-scripting. Word-for-word scripts produce stiff, unnatural content that defeats the purpose of UGC. Give creators the guardrails &mdash; messaging, tone, format &mdash; and let them bring their own voice.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08 Red Flags When Hiring -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="red-flags">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Red Flags</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Red flags when hiring UGC creators</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Watch for these warning signs before committing budget</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Most red flags become obvious only after reviewing a portfolio</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">A paid test video ($50&ndash;$100) is the best vetting tool</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Not every UGC creator delivers quality work. These are the warning signs that indicate a creator may not be the right fit.</p>

        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>Stock-looking content</strong> &mdash; if the portfolio looks like it could be a stock video, it will not perform as UGC. The whole point is authenticity. Overly lit, staged, perfect-angle content signals a misunderstanding of the format.</li>
          <li><strong>Inability to improvise</strong> &mdash; creators who can only read scripts word-for-word will produce stiff content. Ask for a 30-second test without a script. If they cannot speak naturally about a product, they are not a UGC creator.</li>
          <li><strong>Missed deadlines</strong> &mdash; reliability is non-negotiable in volume production. One missed deadline in a Canvas UGC program creates cascading gaps. Ask about their production capacity before committing.</li>
          <li><strong>No portfolio diversity</strong> &mdash; a portfolio with 10 videos that all look the same suggests limited range. Good UGC creators can adapt their style, tone, and format to different brands and briefs.</li>
          <li><strong>Over-polished aesthetic</strong> &mdash; professional lighting setups, studio backgrounds, and cinematic color grading are red flags for UGC. The content should look like it was made by a real person in their real environment.</li>
          <li><strong>Poor communication</strong> &mdash; slow responses, unclear questions, inability to confirm specs. If communication is difficult during hiring, it will be worse during production under deadlines.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">The most reliable way to vet a creator is a paid test video. Send a simple brief and a small payment ($50&ndash;$100). The test reveals production quality, communication style, turnaround time, and ability to follow a brief &mdash; all in one data point.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 Working with Creator Networks -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="networks">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Creator Networks</div>
